For a copy of all correspondence exchanged between the minister of public works, the deputy minister, or any officer or clerk of the department, and any person or persons in the county of Pictou, referring to or in any way dealing with the subject of repairs or renewals of the repairs to or the extension of the pier at Bailey's Brook and repairs to railway wharves, Pictou; also for a copy of all payrolls and accounts in connection with both of above works;
2.	For a return showing the names of the several foremen and all persons employed thereon, the number of days of employment, the rate of pay and the total amount paid each;
3.	For a return showing materials used in such repairs or renewals, particularly the kinds and quantities of timber, lumber, iron and steel, and fastenings, and the prices paid therefor;
4.	For a return showing other materials, their quantities and the prices paid therefor, the total cost of same in each case and the names of the parties from whom purchased and the amounts paid to each;
5.	For a statement showing the date when the work on each of these repair operations begun, when operations ceased, whether repairs are completed in each case, and if not, what proportion of the work has been done, when the work will be resumed and when completed.

Mr. ROBERT K. SMITH (Cumberland) moved:
Whereas in the year 1870 the federal government appointed a canal commission to report on proposed canal works in Canada;
And whereas said commission reported in 1871, dividing the proposed works into four classes and set forth "that all the works under the head of first class are really of so great importance and so essential to the welfare and prosperity of the whole country that they should be undertaken and proceeded with";
And whereas the proposed canal across the isthmus of Chignecto was placed in category of one of the first class projects;
430	COMMONS
Chignccto Canal-Mr. Smith (Cumberland)
And whereas the said proposed canal across the isthmus of Chignecto is the only proposed canal undertaking of the first class that has not been proceeded with;
And whereas said undertaking is of great importance and essential to the welfare and prosperity of the whole of Canada;
Resolved, that in the opinion of this house, it is advisable that the government of Canada take immediate steps to further investigate said project as to feasibility, cost of construction, economic and national advantages to be gained bv the construction of a ship canal across the isthmus of Chignecto to connect the waters of the bay of Fundy with the waters of the gulf of St. Lawrence and further that the government be urged to make these surveys and investigations with the least possible delay;
And that when the surveys, estimates as to cost of construction and economic and national advantages are satisfactorily established the government should take the necessary steps to proceed with the construction of said canal at the earliest possible date.
He said: Mr. Speaker, in moving this resolution it is my intention to approach the discussion of it in the nature of a broad, national project. I am urging the government to investigate the matter in connection with the proposed construction of a canal across the isthmus of Chignecto, joining the waters of the bay of Fundy with the waters of the gulf of St. Lawrence, for the benefit of shorter, cheaper, safer and more advantageous transportation facilities from the national viewpoint.
May I first give the house a brief outline of the historical aspect of the undertaking for the purpose of refreshing in the minds of some hon. members the unwarranted delay of governments in the past in regard to its construction. Before, however, going into that phase, may I point out the geographical location. In this connection I have distributed on the desks of the members a rough sketch showing the saving in distance from many points in Canada to places inside as well as outside our country. The isthmus of Chignecto connects Nova Scotia with New Brunswick, and is about eighteen miles from water to water. The proposed canal route through the isthmus would pass through a soft clay soil, with less than one mile of rock formation, and that of a loose, gravelly content. The location, from the standpoint of dredging, excavating and cost of construction is about as ideal as could be found anywhere, using for some distance the channels of small rivers and lakes, and the engineering difficulties so far as I know present no serious problems. But I feel that that is a matter which I am not qualified to go into at this moment. It is a matter for thorough investigation and modem expert opinion. Surveys for the proposed canal were made as far back as 1825, over one hundred years ago. Several surveys have been made since that

time, I believe five in all. A resolution that I shall read to the house in a few minutes will give a clear outline on that point.
May I say that when the conferences preceding confederation were being held in Quebec and Charlottetown this question was discussed, and the impression left with the delegates from the maritime provinces was that this would be one of the transportation services that we would secure on entering confederation. Senator Dickie from Cumberland and Senator Botsford from Westmorland frequently testified to this point.
In 1870 a royal commission was appointed by the then government, consisting of Sir Hugh Allan and1 others, and that commission recommended to the government of Canada that it proceed with the least possible delay to construct certain specified canals, which they classified in the order in which they were to be built, and I am happy to inform the house that the so-called Baie Verte canal across the isthmus of Chignecto was placed in the list of first-class canals to be constructed without delay, but I am unhappy to say that it was the only one of the first-class canals projects so reoommended that was not proceeded with. I wish to quote some findings of the commission touching on the matter of the construction of the Baie Verte canal. Before doing so may I say that this was a royal commission composed of the most representative commercial men of Canada selected from the _ various provinces. The Baie Verte canal, after full inquiry and examination, was placed by them in the first rank of canals to be constructed by the Dominion government. The following is extracted from the report of the commission submitted to the government of the day:
The growth of intercolonial trade depends on cheap transit, since the merchandise passing between the maritime provinces and Ontario must be of a bulky character, requiring large vessels and rapid dispatch to be really profitable. When a propellor can go direct with a cargo of coal, or other produce of the eastern provinces, to Kingston and Toronto, and there get a return freight of flour, barley and other western produce, intercolonial trade will have entered on a new era.
When Nova Scotia coal of the best description can be supplied abundantly and cheaply to -western ports, a great impulse will necessarily be given to the transfer of the trade of the St. Lawrence and lakes to screw steamers, a transfer already taking place as we have previously shown.
With the canals enlarged, coal freighters would be reduced to the minimum point-a lake propellor would always bring back from the lower ports a cargo of coal, rather than come empty-just as the English timber ships have been accustomed to bring the same articles instead of ballast.

Chignecto Canal-Mr. Smith (Cumberland)
Inseparably connected with the growth of intercolonial trade is the construction of the Baie Verte canal across the isthmus, connecting the provinces of Nova Scotia and New Brunswick. The advantages that must accrue, not merely to the Dominion as a whole, but to the commerce of the maritime provinces, are so clearly pointed out by the boards of trade of all the leading cities of Canada, and by men interested in the development of our commercial interests,-hot simply the merchants of St. John and other places in the locality of the proposed canal, but merchants of Hamilton, Toronto, Ottawa, Montreal and Quebec; that it is superfluous for the commissioners to do more than briefly refer to a few salient features of the scheme.
